17:["$","main",null,{"id":"top","children":[["$","header",null,{"className":"border-b border-ink/10 bg-surface/40","children":["$","div",null,{"className":"mx-auto max-w-5xl px-5 py-10 sm:px-8","children":[["$","$Ld",null,{"href":"/operating-system","className":"inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 text-sm font-semibold text-mute transition hover:text-accent","children":"← All chapters"}],["$","div",null,{"className":"mt-5 flex items-center gap-2 text-sm font-semibold text-accent","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"rounded-full bg-accent/12 px-2.5 py-0.5","children":"Chapter 14"}],["$","span",null,{"className":"text-mute","children":["· ",16," min read · illustrated"]}]]}],["$","h1",null,{"className":"mt-3 font-hand text-5xl font-bold leading-tight text-ink sm:text-6xl","children":"Race Conditions & the Critical Section"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"mt-2 text-lg text-mute","children":"What breaks the instant two threads touch the same data, and the exact problem every lock exists to solve"}],["$","div",null,{"className":"mt-6 flex max-w-2xl flex-col gap-3","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/85","children":"We ended the last part with a warning. Threads share an address space, the same heap, the same globals, the same counters, and that sharing is what makes them fast and what makes them dangerous. This chapter is where the danger becomes concrete. The moment two threads read and write the same piece of memory without coordination, the outcome stops depending only on your code and starts depending on the exact, unpredictable interleaving of instructions the scheduler happens to choose. That is a race condition, and it is the single most infamous class of bug in our profession."}],["$","p","1",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/85","children":"What makes races so vicious is that the code looks obviously correct. A line like count++ reads as one indivisible step, so it never occurs to us that another thread could slip in halfway through it. But it can, and when it does, updates vanish silently, no crash, no error, just a total that is quietly, stubbornly wrong, and only sometimes. This chapter takes that one innocent line apart to show you precisely where the gap is."}],["$","p","2",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/85","children":"Then we build the vocabulary that the rest of Part C is written in: the critical section, mutual exclusion, and the three requirements any correct solution must meet. We will try one honest, naive fix and watch it fail, which tells us why hardware and real locks (the next chapters) are not optional. Throughout we keep one eye on the engineering payoff, lost updates in web handlers, check-then-act bugs, and the direct line from all of this to why databases need locks and MVCC."}]]}]]}]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"mx-auto flex max-w-6xl gap-10 px-5 py-12 sm:px-8","children":[["$","nav",null,{"className":"sticky top-8 hidden h-fit w-56 shrink-0 lg:block","children":[["$","p",null,{"className":"mb-3 font-hand text-xl font-bold text-ink","children":"On this page"}],["$","ol",null,{"className":"flex flex-col gap-1.5 text-sm","children":[["$","li","01-when-sharing-goes-wrong",{"children":["$","a",null,{"href":"#01-when-sharing-goes-wrong","className":"flex gap-2 rounded-md px-2 py-1 text-mute transition hover:bg-accent/10 hover:text-accent","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"text-accent/70","children":"01"}],["$","span",null,{"className":"leading-snug","children":"When sharing goes wrong"}]]}]}],["$","li","02-anatomy-of-a-race",{"children":["$","a",null,{"href":"#02-anatomy-of-a-race","className":"flex gap-2 rounded-md px-2 py-1 text-mute transition hover:bg-accent/10 hover:text-accent","children":["$L21","$L22"]}]}],"$L23","$L24","$L25","$L26","$L27"]}]]}],"$L28"]}],"$L29"]}]
